[
    {
        "content": "<p>HashSet has <code>∈</code> while it lacks <code>∋</code></p>\n<div class=\"codehilite\" data-code-language=\"Lean4\"><pre><span></span><code><span class=\"kn\">import</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Std</span>\n\n<span class=\"kn\">def</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">s</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Std</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">HashSet</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Nat</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">{</span><span class=\"mi\">1</span><span class=\"o\">,</span><span class=\"mi\">2</span><span class=\"o\">}</span>\n<span class=\"bp\">#</span><span class=\"n\">eval</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">1</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">∈</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">s</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"c1\">-- true</span>\n<span class=\"bp\">#</span><span class=\"n\">eval</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">s</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">∋</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">1</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"c1\">-- error on '∋', expected token</span>\n</code></pre></div>\n<p>IIRC other implementations of Set like in Mathlib have <code>∋</code>, and it would be good for consistency to support the common notation.</p>",
        "id": 580111774,
        "sender_full_name": "Marvin",
        "timestamp": 1773815756
    },
    {
        "content": "<div class=\"codehilite\" data-code-language=\"Lean4\"><pre><span></span><code><span class=\"kn\">import</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Mathlib</span>\n\n<span class=\"kn\">def</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">s</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Set</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Nat</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">{</span><span class=\"mi\">1</span><span class=\"o\">,</span><span class=\"mi\">2</span><span class=\"o\">}</span>\n<span class=\"bp\">#</span><span class=\"n\">check</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">1</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">∈</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">s</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"c1\">-- true</span>\n<span class=\"bp\">#</span><span class=\"n\">check</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">s</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">∋</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">1</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"c1\">-- error on '∋', expected token</span>\n</code></pre></div>\n<p>I don't see any <code>∋</code> in Mathlib, what are you referring to?</p>",
        "id": 580113945,
        "sender_full_name": "Snir Broshi",
        "timestamp": 1773816887
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>You can add it yourself with <code>notation s \"∋\" x =&gt; x ∈ s</code> btw</p>",
        "id": 580114321,
        "sender_full_name": "Snir Broshi",
        "timestamp": 1773817104
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>You could, but you probably shouldn't for the same reasons we don't like using &gt;</p>",
        "id": 580120205,
        "sender_full_name": "Violeta Hernández",
        "timestamp": 1773819858
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>I really thought I had seen it before on Finset or Set... Strange. There is even a popup telling me how to write the symbol using /ni, what's that for if the symbol isn't defined anywhere in std or mathlib?</p>",
        "id": 580122344,
        "sender_full_name": "Marvin",
        "timestamp": 1773820753
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>The symbol shorthands live upstream of mathlib, and so shouldn't limit themselves to only the symbols that mathlib wants</p>",
        "id": 580123709,
        "sender_full_name": "Eric Wieser",
        "timestamp": 1773821227
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Who wrote this popup for how to write it? Are they auto generated?</p>",
        "id": 580126609,
        "sender_full_name": "Marvin",
        "timestamp": 1773822238
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>I believe they are, from <a href=\"https://github.com/leanprover/vscode-lean4/blob/132d329aa8afa3e8508ef77cfdcff112d3b35c88/lean4-unicode-input/src/abbreviations.json#L179\"><code>abbreviations.json</code></a></p>",
        "id": 580135105,
        "sender_full_name": "Snir Broshi",
        "timestamp": 1773824840
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Here's <a href=\"https://github.com/leanprover/vscode-lean4/blob/132d329aa8afa3e8508ef77cfdcff112d3b35c88/vscode-lean4/src/abbreviation/AbbreviationHoverProvider.ts#L39\">the code</a> that creates them</p>",
        "id": 580135518,
        "sender_full_name": "Snir Broshi",
        "timestamp": 1773824973
    },
    {
        "content": "<p><span class=\"user-mention silent\" data-user-id=\"459227\">Violeta Hernández</span> <a href=\"#narrow/channel/270676-lean4/topic/Std.2EHashSet.20missing.20.E2.88.8B/near/580120205\">schrieb</a>:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>You could, but you probably shouldn't for the same reasons we don't like using &gt;</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>What are these reasons anyway?<br>\nIs it that an <code>abbrev</code> is still syntactically different from its unabbreviated term?<br>\nIf we made <code>&gt;</code> a <code>notation</code> instead, would that solve this?</p>",
        "id": 580151982,
        "sender_full_name": "Moritz Roos",
        "timestamp": 1773829441
    },
    {
        "content": "<p><span class=\"user-mention silent\" data-user-id=\"933054\">Snir Broshi</span> <a href=\"#narrow/channel/270676-lean4/topic/Std.2EHashSet.20missing.20.E2.88.8B/near/580113945\">schrieb</a>:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<div class=\"codehilite\" data-code-language=\"Lean4\"><pre><span></span><code><span class=\"kn\">import</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Mathlib</span>\n\n<span class=\"kn\">def</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">s</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Set</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Nat</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">{</span><span class=\"mi\">1</span><span class=\"o\">,</span><span class=\"mi\">2</span><span class=\"o\">}</span>\n<span class=\"bp\">#</span><span class=\"n\">check</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">1</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">∈</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">s</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"c1\">-- true</span>\n<span class=\"bp\">#</span><span class=\"n\">check</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">s</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">∋</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">1</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"c1\">-- error on '∋', expected token</span>\n</code></pre></div>\n<p>I don't see any <code>∋</code> in Mathlib, what are you referring to?</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>I remember very faintly that once there was a discussion about making ∋ (or maybe it was <code>\\supseteq</code>?) work in calc, so maybe we did have it at some point?</p>",
        "id": 580152254,
        "sender_full_name": "Moritz Roos",
        "timestamp": 1773829516
    },
    {
        "content": "<p><span class=\"user-mention silent\" data-user-id=\"602461\">Moritz R</span> <a href=\"#narrow/channel/270676-lean4/topic/Std.2EHashSet.20missing.20.E2.88.8B/near/580151982\">said</a>:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>What are these reasons anyway?<br>\nIs it that an <code>abbrev</code> is still syntactically different from its unabbreviated term?<br>\nIf we made <code>&gt;</code> a <code>notation</code> instead, would that solve this?</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Yes, an <code>abbrev</code> is still syntactically different from its unabbreviated term, and this has some consequences for tactics like <code>rw</code> and <code>simp</code>.</p>\n<p>If you make it notation, it's hard to ensure that if I type <code>x &lt; y</code> then Lean prints <code>x &lt; y</code> and if I type <code>y &gt; x</code> then Lean prints <code>y &gt; x</code>. Typically, notation is always (or never) used to print the expression it represents, and that would mean that if I type <code>a &lt; b</code>, Lean will print that expression as <code>b &gt; a</code>.<br>\n(This is probably fixable by inserting metadata into the expression to record how it was written, but it's nontrivial).</p>",
        "id": 580154993,
        "sender_full_name": "Floris van Doorn",
        "timestamp": 1773830192
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>I see, thanks!</p>",
        "id": 580176301,
        "sender_full_name": "Moritz Roos",
        "timestamp": 1773836375
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Still a bit sad that we can't really currently deal with users typing <code>&gt;</code> <span aria-label=\"smiling face with tear\" class=\"emoji emoji-1f972\" role=\"img\" title=\"smiling face with tear\">:smiling_face_with_tear:</span></p>",
        "id": 580176516,
        "sender_full_name": "Moritz Roos",
        "timestamp": 1773836431
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>We can deal with writing <code>&gt;</code>. In mathlib we decided on the convention to always use <code>&lt;</code> because it is more convenient, avoiding having to do rewrites with <code>gt_iff_lt</code>. But downstream users should feel free to use <code>&gt;</code> if they like.</p>",
        "id": 580222374,
        "sender_full_name": "Jovan Gerbscheid",
        "timestamp": 1773847204
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>(and it is still used in some cases, eg <code>\\eps &gt; 0</code>)</p>",
        "id": 580227027,
        "sender_full_name": "Eric Rodriguez",
        "timestamp": 1773848295
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>(one could even argue that we chose the wrong thing, as we can't write &lt; in binders, but we can always write &gt; in binders)</p>",
        "id": 580227321,
        "sender_full_name": "Eric Rodriguez",
        "timestamp": 1773848366
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>This issue is addressed by <a href=\"https://github.com/leanprover-community/mathlib4/pull/32985\">#32985</a>, but unfortunately it got some push-back</p>",
        "id": 580227985,
        "sender_full_name": "Jovan Gerbscheid",
        "timestamp": 1773848494
    }
]